## TIFF through WIC (same day)

The user put the 108 MP photos into test\images\tiff as TIFF (IrfanView): 12000 x 9000, 24-bit RGB, 9 strips of 1024 rows; `compressed` = LZW with horizontal predictor (53–60 MB), `uncompressed` = 324 MB each. No embedded preview. Until now TIFF went through BGRABitmap / FPReadTiff: always the full image (432 MB in memory, also for preloads), probably slow, not cancellable.

Now (with `UseWIC=1`) TIFF is read by WIC straight from the file (uWicDecoder.DecodeFileWithWic), in bands of 1024 rows (whole strips), with a cancel check between bands:
- Screen quality: the bands are averaged down by a whole factor while they arrive; only a screen-size bitmap is allocated. Then shrunk exactly to the window's fit (drawn 1:1).
- Full: the whole bitmap plus a screen copy, as before.
- WIC can't read a file: FPReadTiff as before. Out of memory: error entry.
Note: the current TIFF is decoded twice (quick view, then full after RefineDelayMs or on zoom), like JPEG; for LZW each pass costs a full decompression.

To check: D line / timing.csv on both folders (decode ms of `screen` and `full`), and the Phase D test: zoom to 1600 % on a TIFF and pan (smooth?), D line "sharpening n / 108".

## TIFF: first measurements and three fixes (same day)

Measured before the WIC build (old reader, uncompressed 108 MP TIFF): full decode 1712 ms, screen copy 680 ms, GPU per 1024 tile 6.3 ms pixels + 13.1 ms mipmaps (the Day 16 fix: about 8x faster uploads). Hamana needs a little under 3 s for the same file.
After the WIC build: screen decode 2696 ms, screen copy 344 ms, latency 3096 ms, cache 17 MB instead of ~430 MB per TIFF — but "jobs 3 waiting, 3 of 4 workers busy": three preloads were reading other 324 MB files from the same disk at the same time.

1. **Preloads give the disk to the display job.** The I/O gate so far only held back the folder scanner. Now preload decodes wait at the gate too: before reading a JPEG / other file, between the WIC bands of a TIFF, and every 32 output rows of the quick TIFF reader (IOGate.YieldToDisplay: checks cancel every 50 ms, gives up after 5 s so nothing can hang).
2. **Uncompressed TIFF quick view reads only the rows it needs** (new unit uTiffQuick): own minimal IFD reader; for uncompressed, strip, 8-bit, chunky RGB / grey files each row's place in the file is known, so the quick view reads 2 rows of every S (a quarter and three quarters into each group) and averages all columns: 81 MB instead of 324 MB at S = 8. Checked with a Python copy of the loop on IMG_20241126_200828.tif (correct picture). Other TIFFs (LZW etc.) go through WIC as before.
3. **Final resize step own bilinear** (uImageScaling.BilinearResize) instead of BGRABitmap's resampler: the 1500 x 1125 -> 1440 x 1080 step took 344 ms; bilinear with 8-bit fractions, matches Pillow's bilinear within 0.4 levels on average. Also speeds up the JPEG quick views' "screen copy".

To check: the same TIFF again (screen decode, screen copy, latency), the LZW folder, and whether "jobs waiting" still slows the first image.

## Drag and drop (end of the session)

A file or folder dropped on the MView window opens it, like a command line argument: its folder becomes the browsing root. Dropped on the settings editor, it starts the viewer with it. Several files: only the first counts. (TForm.AllowDropFiles / OnDropFiles in uMainForm.)

## Open for next session

- **Loading times got higher after the TIFF changes** (reported by the user, no numbers yet). Get the D line / timing.csv first. Suspects:
  - Windows' file cache: the earlier 1712 ms may have been a file already in memory, a fresh read of 324 MB from disk is slower; compare a second opening of the same file.
  - YieldToDisplay: a preload that promoted to display job keeps PriorityRead = False and may now wait at the gate behind the other display job (preview / screen of the same file), up to 5 s per band.
  - uTiffQuick: 2 x 1125 separate seeks + 36 KB reads; on a hard disk the seeks can cost more than reading everything in order. Try one row per group, or read whole strips in order and skip rows in memory.
  - The WIC quick view for JPEG (UseWICQuickView): compare with 0.
- Spec update for Phase E once the measurements are in.

## Measured after all fixes (timing.csv, 14:22-14:26) — better than it felt

Uncompressed 108 MP TIFF (324 MB):
| | before today | now |
|---|---|---|
| quick view decode | 2696 ms (1712 full, old reader) | 168-368 ms (row-skipping reader) |
| screen copy / final resize | 344-680 ms | 24-120 ms |
| full decode (WIC) | 1712 ms (old reader) | 1432-1480 ms |
| next image from cache | - | 8-48 ms |

LZW TIFF (55 MB): quick view decode 4.4-4.7 s, 9.2 s when two ran at once; from cache 24-48 ms. This is the slow case left (Hamana: just under 3 s). Suspect: WIC's TIFF codec decodes a 1024-row strip again for each band, or LZW through WIC is just slow. Next: time one CopyPixels of the whole image vs banded; else an own LZW + predictor decoder (sequential, could also average while decoding).
